                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "68b75dc3-3323-4e24-baec-9fae74980e6c",
                        "source_entry_id": "71202741-f741-3161-9275-ab48ae401058",
                        "target_lang_word": "bough",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "bough",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "daf5bba8-f721-389a-81a8-22f3b798018d",
                                        "entry_id": "80c2fd14-91bb-3023-9946-1deca37ea065",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A tree-branch, usually a primary one directly attached to the trunk."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e669e206-8923-46e8-ba94-16bd3b77c7fd",
                                                "sense_id": "daf5bba8-f721-389a-81a8-22f3b798018d",
                                                "text": "When the bough breaks, the cradle will fall. (Rock-a-bye Baby)"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "939cf3bf-6023-4b55-8c2c-247b7c7411a2",
                                                "sense_id": "daf5bba8-f721-389a-81a8-22f3b798018d",
                                                "text": "Where the Bee ſucks, there ſuck I, / In a Cowſlips bell, I lie, / There I cowch when Owles doe crie, / On the Batts backe I doe flie / after Sommer merrily. / Merrily, merrily, ſhall I liue now / Vnder the bloſſom that hangs on the Bow."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5633cd8f-f239-49cc-b6b0-48275d5243d8",
                                                "sense_id": "daf5bba8-f721-389a-81a8-22f3b798018d",
                                                "text": "When thou beateſt thine oliue tree thou shalt not goe ouer the boughes againe: it shall be for the ſtranger, for the fatherleſſe, and for the widow."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5cc509c2-87f1-4d16-90b2-20fc7037834c",
                                                "sense_id": "daf5bba8-f721-389a-81a8-22f3b798018d",
                                                "text": "[Y]ou are to faſten that line to any bow neer to a hole where a Pike is, or is likely to lye, or to have a haunt, […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9cbc903c-3480-4e01-917f-93faf7547dda",
                                                "sense_id": "daf5bba8-f721-389a-81a8-22f3b798018d",
                                                "text": "When the Corn was ſow'd, I had no Harrow, but was forced to go over it my ſelf, and drag a great heavy Bough of a Tree over it, to Scratch it, as it may be call'd, rather than Rake or harrow it."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "52e3edbd-0d79-4c29-9972-c53b0488d2c2",
                                                "sense_id": "daf5bba8-f721-389a-81a8-22f3b798018d",
                                                "text": "Ah, happy, happy boughs! that cannot shed / Your leaves, nor ever bid the Spring adieu; […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cb110045-7ed5-482c-bd65-381c6ef119d5",
                                                "sense_id": "daf5bba8-f721-389a-81a8-22f3b798018d",
                                                "text": "Now we plunged into a deep shade with the boughs lacing each other overhead, and crossed dainty, rustic bridges over the cold trout-streams, the boards giving back the clatter of our horses' feet: or anon we shot into a clearing, with a colored glimpse of the lake and its curving shore far below us."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3b4e7d16-9d30-4ab9-8d54-398f771f62e4",
                                                "sense_id": "daf5bba8-f721-389a-81a8-22f3b798018d",
                                                "text": "[T]he creature [a Wagler's viper (Tropidolaemus wagleri)] is arboreal and feeds on birds. This extremely agile prey it is able to capture with ease, because it has developed a prehensile tail whereby it is able to take a secure grip of a bough, leaving the rest of the body free to be instantly uncoiled as the fatal dart on the victim is made. The green colour of the young snake is a protective garment, enabling it to lie concealed among the smaller green boughs. Later, with increased bulk, older and therefore black boughs have to bear the weight of the body, against which a green body would be somewhat conspicuous, or would at any rate excite suspicion."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9dad0df6-a1e2-4bf7-9d67-a86a18d9047f",
                                                "sense_id": "daf5bba8-f721-389a-81a8-22f3b798018d",
                                                "text": "A pair of birds settle on the bough above them, murmuring together, ready to roost."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0b316463-f362-43e8-ab99-f6e0ce42c2e3",
                                                "sense_id": "daf5bba8-f721-389a-81a8-22f3b798018d",
                                                "text": "Desperate to stop before hurling herself off the edge of the cliff, she grabbed for a nearby tree branch, but the spindly bough snapped off in her hands."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "46d96f71-401c-3144-8d0f-2c0b01197c4e",
                                        "entry_id": "80c2fd14-91bb-3023-9946-1deca37ea065",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A gallows."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "figuratively",
                                            "obsolete",
                                            "poetic"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d21ae0e1-7485-4388-b9a0-217b35a214d5",
                                                "sense_id": "46d96f71-401c-3144-8d0f-2c0b01197c4e",
                                                "text": "It was vſed of auncient time in Gauelkind land, & hath receiued the allowance and iudgement of a good and lawfull cuſtome, that if the huſband be attainted and executed for a felonie by him committed, yet ſhall his wife for the ſolace of her loſſe and deſolation haue her dowrie of his land, and alſo the heire ſhall inherite the ſame according to that olde ſaying: The father to the bough, & the ſonne to the plough, […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "481fc90b-c00f-4ee5-9631-b2c7f51b7cf9",
                                                "sense_id": "46d96f71-401c-3144-8d0f-2c0b01197c4e",
                                                "text": "\"No need,\" he said, \"long words to make, / And little heed we thy lies now, / But if she doom thee to the bough.[\"]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "dc96dd2c-e3d6-4036-8756-d84c110230dc",
                        "source_entry_id": "71202741-f741-3161-9275-ab48ae401058",
